  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Determine the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1.$\n\nThe domain is $x\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "The domain of $y=\\sqrt{x}$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the domain of $f(x)=4\\sqrt{7+x}+1,$ we replace $x$ with $7+x$ in the above inequality. This gives\n\n$$\n7+x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\in [-7,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can also see that this is true from the graph of the function. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14239347/assets/1b5b24146a009936bb2fab85afa4f40b888881e4d6978fb76e76e7180b2cd83d.png)",
